<section id="kvc-koko" class="kv">
  <h2>The virus no scanner could see</h2>
  <p class="lead">In 1993 a virus called Koko was spreading across PCs in Egypt, and it could wipe a hard disk. No antivirus on the market could find it. So Yasser and Ebrahim El-Etr took it apart by hand, learned its secrets, and turned them against it.</p>
  <p>Koko lived in memory. Run one infected program and Koko stayed behind, like a background service that never stops. From then on it slipped a copy of itself into every program the PC ran. It signed each one with its name, <code>KoKo (1)</code>. Most days it did nothing else you could see. Two days a year, it struck.</p>
  <Steps
    title="How Koko took over a PC"
    steps={[
      `A clean PC. Every program asks DOS, the system, for everything through one door: <b><a class="term" href="${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/glossary/#int-21h" data-term="int-21h">slot 21h</a></b>. Think of one API endpoint that every app on the machine calls.`,
      'Someone runs a program that carries Koko. <b>Koko moves into memory and stays there</b>, even after that program ends.',
      'Koko writes its own address into slot 21h, so every request reaches Koko first. It keeps the real DOS address for itself, in <b>slot 3</b>, a slot normal programs never use.',
      'Now every program that starts gets a copy of Koko, signed <code>KoKo (1)</code>.',
      'On <b>February 17</b>, Koko shows its warning on the screen.',
      'On <b>July 29</b>, the warning comes again, and the data on the hard disk is lost. So is the data on any floppy used that day.',
    ]}
  >
